# URI parsing
# -qt6 is load-bearing under the kde flavor. uriparser declares
#     REQUIRED_USE="qt6? ( doc )"
# and the project disables `doc` globally (the REMOVED group in make.conf), so the
# two cannot both hold. The flavor's `use_prefer: add: [qt6]` is GLOBAL -- it
# turns qt6 on in any package declaring the flag, including a URI parser reached
# through dev-lang/php. php asks for --with-external-uriparser: it wants the
# library, not the Qt binding. Found by step12-kde-resolve, which failed with
# "unmet requirements" until this line existed (lab, 2026-09-12).
dev-libs/uriparser -qt6

# Qt6 bindings nobody asks for.
#
# A flavor's `use_prefer: add: [qt6]` is GLOBAL: it turns qt6 on in every package
# declaring the flag, which under kde means libraries acquire a Qt binding or a
# Qt test utility that nothing in the plan uses -- and each one drags
# dev-qt/qtbase:6 into a dependency chain where it does not belong. That is how
# step12-kde-resolve hit the cycle
#     qtbase -> cups -> xdg-utils -> xmlto -> w3m -> imlib2
#            -> libheif -> ffmpeg -> libv4l[qt6] -> qtbase
# whose cheapest cut, by far, was libv4l[-qt6]: that flag exists to build qv4l2,
# a graphical capture-test tool. The six other cuts Portage offered each removed
# real function (webcam capture in ffmpeg, X printing, HEIF, HEVC).
#
# Checked one by one against the kde plan: no package requires any of these with
# [qt6]. app-text/poppler is NOT here for that reason -- kde-frameworks/
# kfilemetadata does require poppler[qt6] (lab, 2026-09-13).
# NOT here, and each was put back after being wrongly swept up:
#   dev-libs/appstream[qt6]  <- kde-plasma/plasma-workspace (via plasma-meta)
#   media-libs/mlt[qt6]      <- kde-apps/kdenlive (in sets/kde)
#   app-text/poppler[qt6]    <- app-editors/kile, app-office/calligra, ...
#
# METHOD NOTE, learned twice in one session. Searching for consumers of
# `cat/pkg[flag]` must allow a VERSION between the name and the bracket:
# kdenlive writes `>=media-libs/mlt-7.40.0[qt6]`, and a pattern of the shape
# `cat/pkg(:slot)?\[` silently matches nothing. The first sweep missed it; so
# did the second, which additionally searched only the TRUNCATED plan rather
# than the whole tree, so kdenlive was not even in the corpus.
media-gfx/graphviz -qt6
media-libs/libv4l -qt6
media-libs/opencv -qt6
media-libs/suil -qt6
net-dns/avahi -qt6

